# Device access for hardware-ui.
#
# Install:  cp packaging/70-hardware-ui.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#           udevadm control --reload-rules && udevadm trigger
#
# The application runs as your user and never as root. These two lines are what make that
# possible; see docs/INSTALL.md for what each module needs.
#
# Matched on the node *type* rather than per vendor and product. A per-device rule would mean a
# new line for every device bought and one per module; this covers every vendor at once, including
# hardware no module supports yet.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" hands the device to whoever is logged in at the seat, which is the correct
# semantics for desktop hardware: it follows the session rather than granting a group permanent
# access to every keyboard and monitor on the machine.

# Vendor control protocols ride on hidraw: Jabra (usage page 0xFF00), Poly Deckard, Logitech
# HID++, FIDO2/CTAP security keys, Dell docks.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# Creative sound cards and headphone amplifiers speak a vendor protocol over a CDC-ACM function,
# which is claimed with libusb rather than opened as a node -- so unlike every other rule here,
# this one has to name a vendor. Scoped to Creative Technology (041e) precisely because a blanket
# `SUBSYSTEM=="usb"` rule would hand every USB device on the machine to the logged-in session.
#
# On kernels without `cdc_acm` nothing is bound to those interfaces, so libusb claims them without
# detaching a driver and without disturbing ALSA, which owns only the audio interfaces.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="041e", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# 8BitDo's Xbox controllers speak GIP on a vendor-specific interface and expose no hidraw at all,
# so the interface has to be claimed with libusb -- the same situation as Creative, and the same
# reason this rule has to name a vendor rather than a node type.
#
# Claiming detaches xpad, which is put back on the way out. While a configuration read or write is
# in flight the controller stops acting as a gamepad for about a second.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="2dc8", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# DDC/CI for monitors. The i2c-dev kernel module must also be loaded, and membership of the `i2c`
# group is what ddcutil itself needs -- see docs/INSTALL.md.
SUBSYSTEM=="i2c-dev", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# Razer devices are deliberately absent: this application talks to the OpenRazer daemon, which
# owns the hardware through its own kernel drivers and ships its own rules. Add yourself to
# `plugdev` for those instead.
